WEBVTT

NOTE Generated for Agent OS overview video

1.1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.955
Every day, thousands of developers use AI coding agents to build software. But there's a problem: every session starts from zero.

1.3
00:00:06.822 --> 00:00:10.233
No shared context. No memory of past work.

1.5
00:00:13.645 --> 00:00:19.188
Agents that repeat the same mistakes over and over. Agent OS solves this.

1.7
00:00:20.468 --> 00:00:23.880
It's an agent-agnostic harness — the operating system that AI coding agents run on.

2.1
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:33.212
Imagine working with a developer who forgets everything at the end of each day. That's what AI coding agents do today.

2.3
00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:37.177
No shared memory between sessions. No way to coordinate across different agent tools.

2.5
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:46.190
No enforced rules that persist. And no confidence that your agents are actually following your project's standards.

2.7
00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:50.036
Agent OS changes this by giving agents persistent, shared context.

3.1
00:00:50.536 --> 00:01:03.303
Agent OS is a CLI-first harness that orchestrates any AI coding agent as a first-class citizen in a shared, memory-driven system. Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Droid — they all participate equally.

3.3
00:00:59.761 --> 00:01:04.703
This is a harness, not a framework. Frameworks are libraries you import.

3.5
00:01:08.987 --> 00:01:13.600
The harness owns the outcome, learns from mistakes, and gets better every cycle.

4.1
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:22.465
Agent OS has four main components. The Workspace Router detects the active workspace and loads its rules, preventing cross-domain contamination.

4.3
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:36.770
The Memory Layer combines short-term events, semantic search, and graph relationships. The Agent Communication Protocol, or ACP, is a durable task ledger that makes agent work resumable and auditable.

4.5
00:01:35.181 --> 00:01:40.452
And the Agent Harness launches the right runtime with the right tools and context.

5.1
00:01:40.952 --> 00:01:47.348
Here's how a request flows through Agent OS. First, a task arrives from the user.

5.3
00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:55.449
The router detects the workspace and task type. Relevant memory is recalled and merged into a context bundle.

5.5
00:01:54.597 --> 00:02:01.846
The right agent, model, and tools are dispatched. And finally, useful outcomes are captured for later promotion.

5.7
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.978
Every step is tracked and auditable.

6.1
00:02:05.332 --> 00:02:15.204
The memory layer is what gives agents continuity. The short-term tier uses SQLite with full-text search and is always on — no external services needed.

6.3
00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:21.128
The semantic tier uses Pinecone for cross-session vector search. And the graph tier uses Neo4j for relationship-based queries.

6.5
00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:27.052
You start with local-only memory and scale up when you need it.

7.1
00:02:27.552 --> 00:02:35.464
Knowledge doesn't just accumulate — it goes through a promotion pipeline. During a session, candidate observations are captured.

7.3
00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:51.288
Promotion jobs compress conversations into durable facts, deduplicate near-matches, attach provenance, and write only the useful pieces into longer-term memory. The result: agents start with better context each time, without memory becoming an unbounded transcript dump.

8.1
00:02:51.788 --> 00:03:01.714
ACP is the durable task ledger. Each task gets a run directory with its objective, current state, timestamps, and an append-only transition log.

8.3
00:03:02.319 --> 00:03:11.814
Tasks flow from queued to claimed, running, review, and finally succeed or fail. This makes agent work resumable, inspectable, cancelable, and auditable.

8.5
00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:18.116
Different agent roles — Explorer, Architect, Executor, Reviewer — handle the work they're best suited for.

9.1
00:03:18.616 --> 00:03:27.216
Agent OS orchestrates multiple agents through configurable workflows. MOE panels combine different LLM providers for parallel reasoning.

9.3
00:03:27.048 --> 00:03:35.142
Swarms dispatch concurrent agents on independent subtasks. Council mode brings multiple perspectives to a single question.

9.5
00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:43.912
And Red Team mode provides adversarial review. All orchestration is provider-agnostic — no lock-in at any layer.

10.1
00:03:44.412 --> 00:03:53.077
Getting started is straightforward. Clone the repository, run the install script, add your LLM provider API key, and source the configuration.

10.3
00:03:54.645 --> 00:04:04.548
The default install works entirely locally with zero external services. You can be up and running with persistent agent memory in under five minutes.

10.5
00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:09.996
Optional components like Vault OS for knowledge management and SuperDocs for project documentation are available as add-ons.

11.1
00:04:10.496 --> 00:04:19.008
Agent OS is published under Apache 2.0. It's free for personal use, commercial use, modification, and redistribution.

11.3
00:04:20.344 --> 00:04:28.355
The full harness is open source under the Apache 2.0 license. See the LICENSE file in the repository for details.

11.5
00:04:30.192 --> 00:04:40.040
The open-core includes the full harness: local memory, agent routing, multi-agent orchestration, and all extension contracts. Managed infrastructure and enterprise features are reserved for commercial licensing.

12.1
00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:51.621
Agent OS gives your coding agents what they've been missing: persistent memory, enforced protocols, and the ability to learn and improve together. Every session builds on the last.

12.3
00:04:49.247 --> 00:04:53.996
Every agent contributes to shared knowledge. Visit the repository to get started.

12.5
00:04:57.954 --> 00:05:02.308
Star the project, try the install, and give your agents the operating system they deserve.
